### 1. What It Is
Memoization in React is an optimization technique that caches computation results (`useMemo`), function references (`useCallback`), or component render outputs (`React.memo`) to skip redundant re-rendering when inputs have not changed by shallow equality (`Object.is`).

### 2. Why It Exists & Historical Motivation
In React, whenever a parent component re-renders, all child components re-render recursively by default—even if their props are identical. Furthermore, declaring inline functions or objects creates brand-new memory references on every render, invalidating downstream memoization.

### 3. What Problem It Solves
Prevents unnecessary execution of expensive CPU algorithms (sorting 10,000 items) and prevents pure child components from re-rendering when parent state changes unrelated to them.

### 4. Prerequisites
JavaScript reference types vs primitives, `Object.is` shallow equality, closure mechanics, and React component render lifecycles.

### 5. Syntax & Parameters
const Component = React.memo(MyComponent, arePropsEqual?);
const memoizedValue = useMemo(() => computeExpensiveValue(a, b), [a, b]);
const memoizedCallback = useCallback((arg) => { doSomething(arg); }, [deps]);

### 6. Minimal Working Example
```jsx
import React, { useState, useMemo, useCallback } from "react";

const HeavyChart = React.memo(function HeavyChart({ data, onSelect }) {
  console.log("Rendering HeavyChart");
  return <div onClick={() => onSelect(data.length)}>Points: {data.length}</div>;
});

export function Dashboard() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const [filter, setFilter] = useState("active");

  // 1. Memoize expensive array computation
  const filteredData = useMemo(() => {
    return Array.from({ length: 5000 }, (_, i) => ({ id: i, status: i % 2 === 0 ? "active" : "inactive" }))
      .filter(item => item.status === filter);
  }, [filter]);

  // 2. Stable function reference for child prop
  const handleSelect = useCallback((total) => {
    console.log(`Selected chart with ${total} items`);
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment Counter: {count}</button>
      <HeavyChart data={filteredData} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

### 7. Detailed Line-by-Line Explanation
- Line 3: `React.memo` wraps `HeavyChart`, configuring it to compare incoming `data` and `onSelect` using `Object.is`.
- Line 13: `useMemo` executes the expensive array filter ONLY when `filter` changes, reusing the cached array across counter increments.
- Line 18: `useCallback` returns the exact same function reference between renders because its dependency array is empty (`[]`).
- Line 23: Clicking the button re-renders `Dashboard`. `count` updates, but `filteredData` and `handleSelect` retain stable references.
- Line 24: `HeavyChart` receives identical props; React skips re-rendering its entire Fiber subtree!

### 8. Under-the-Hood Internal Engine Flow
`useMemo` and `useCallback` store memoized tuples `[value, deps]` inside the Fiber node's `memoizedState` linked list. During re-render, React compares previous deps against current deps using `Object.is`. If all match, it returns the cached instance; if any differ, it invokes the factory and updates the slot.

### 9. When to Use It
When a component renders a complex DOM subtree or runs CPU-heavy calculations (>5ms), AND receives props that frequently retain identity, OR when passing callbacks to memoized children or hook dependency arrays.

### 10. When NOT to Use It
Never memoize trivial calculations (e.g. `useMemo(() => a + b, [a, b])`). The memory overhead of storing arrays, closures, and performing dependency comparisons is higher than re-executing trivial operations.

### 11. Common Mistakes & Anti-Patterns
Wrapping a callback in `useCallback` but passing it to an un-memoized child component. If the child is not wrapped in `React.memo`, it re-renders anyway, rendering `useCallback` useless!

### 12. Performance & Accessibility (A11y) Implications
Memoization is not free; it consumes heap memory for dependency arrays and closures. Optimize component architecture (state colocation, composition with `children`) before applying memoization.

