### 1. What It Is
An Error Boundary is a React component that catches JavaScript errors anywhere in its child component tree during rendering, in lifecycle methods, and in constructors, logs those errors, and displays a graceful fallback UI instead of crashing the entire React component tree.

### 2. Why It Exists & Historical Motivation
In React 16+, an uncaught JavaScript error inside a component's render phase causes React to unmount the entire component tree from the DOM, resulting in a blank white screen for the end user.

### 3. What Problem It Solves
Isolates crashes to specific feature widgets (e.g. broken comments feed doesn't break product page or checkout flow), reports telemetry to Sentry/Datadog, and provides one-click retry buttons.

### 4. Prerequisites
React component lifecycles, class components, and JavaScript `try/catch` limitations.

### 5. Syntax & Parameters
class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
  static getDerivedStateFromError(error) { return { hasError: true, error }; }
  componentDidCatch(error, errorInfo) { logToMonitoring(error, errorInfo); }
}

### 6. Minimal Working Example
```jsx
import React from "react";

export class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { hasError: false, error: null };
  }

  static getDerivedStateFromError(error) {
    // Update state so the next render will show the fallback UI.
    return { hasError: true, error };
  }

  componentDidCatch(error, errorInfo) {
    // Send crash telemetry to Datadog / Sentry
    console.error("ErrorBoundary caught an error:", error, errorInfo.componentStack);
  }

  handleReset = () => {
    this.setState({ hasError: false, error: null });
  };

  return() {
    if (this.state.hasError) {
      return (
        <div style={{ padding: "1.5rem", border: "1px solid #ef4444", borderRadius: "8px", background: "#fef2f2" }}>
          <h3 style={{ color: "#991b1b" }}>Something went wrong.</h3>
          <p style={{ color: "#b91c1c" }}>{this.state.error?.message}</p>
          <button onClick={this.handleReset}>Try Again</button>
        </div>
      );
    }
    return this.props.children;
  }
}
```

### 7. Detailed Line-by-Line Explanation
- Line 3: Must be implemented as a Class Component because Hooks do not yet provide equivalents for `getDerivedStateFromError` or `componentDidCatch`.
- Line 9: `static getDerivedStateFromError(error)` is called during the "render" phase after a child throws. It returns new state to render the fallback UI.
- Line 14: `componentDidCatch(error, errorInfo)` runs during the "commit" phase, allowing side-effects like logging to remote APM services.
- Line 19: `handleReset` clears the error state, allowing the child tree to attempt re-rendering when the user clicks "Try Again".

### 8. Under-the-Hood Internal Engine Flow
When an unhandled exception occurs inside a Fiber's render pass, Fiber halts work on that subtree and unwinds the call stack upwards until it finds a Fiber node implementing `componentDidCatch` or `getDerivedStateFromError`. It schedules a high-priority commit rendering the boundary's fallback UI, keeping sibling and parent subtrees completely intact.

### 9. When to Use It
At strategic architectural levels: root application level (to catch fatal crashes), route boundaries (so a failed route shows a 404/500 UI without crashing the shell), and complex third-party widgets (embedded charts, payment iframes).

### 10. When NOT to Use It
Do not expect Error Boundaries to catch: event handler errors (use `try/catch` there), asynchronous code (`setTimeout`, raw `fetch` callbacks), server-side rendering errors, or errors thrown inside the Error Boundary itself.

### 11. Common Mistakes & Anti-Patterns
Assuming Error Boundaries catch async API errors. If `fetch().catch()` fails, it does NOT trigger an Error Boundary unless you explicitly set state with that error during render.

### 12. Performance & Accessibility (A11y) Implications
Render fallback UIs with `role="alert"` and clear focus management so keyboard and screen-reader users are immediately notified of the crash.

### 13. Real-World Production Usage
